// src/v4/extdeps/process.dag
//
// Scope: process model — child process invocation, lifecycle, exit codes, signals.
// Anchor: https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/ (POSIX.1-2024 / IEEE Std 1003.1-2024)
//
// Owns:
//   - RunningProcess / WaitedTerminatedProcess / TerminatedProcess
//     (NO broad lifecycle Process carrier and NO separate exit_code
//     field — each operation boundary uses the lifecycle-specific
//     process carrier that makes impossible states unrepresentable.
//     Shared id/command/parent facts live in ProcessIdentity and flow
//     forward through lifecycle carriers rather than being re-authored.
//     Terminal status lives ONLY inside TerminatedProcessState {
//     termination: Termination }, reached through
//     WaitedTerminatedProcess.state or TerminatedProcess.state.)
//   - ProcessId (PID, PositiveInt — never zero per POSIX)
//   - Termination = Exited(ExitCode) | Signaled(SignalNum)
//     (the ONLY two ways a process ends per POSIX. wait/capture return
//     terminal process carriers whose state carries THIS, never a bare
//     ExitCode: a signal-killed process has no exit code; fabricating one
//     or flattening signal death into a Diagnostic loses the SignalNum.
//     `Running` is structurally excluded from all terminal carriers.)
//   - ExitCode = NonNegativeInt (per POSIX — 0 success, 1-255 conventional)
//   - SignalNum (per POSIX signal table)
//   - Command { program: AbsolutePath, argv0: String, args: List<String>, env: Map<String,String> }
//   - spawn(pt, cmd) -> Result<{table: ProcessTable, process: RunningProcess}, Diagnostic>
//   - wait(spawned)  -> Result<{table: ProcessTable, process: WaitedTerminatedProcess}, Diagnostic>
//     (blocks until terminated)
//   - capture(source) -> Result<{stdout, stderr: FreeMonoid<Byte>, table: ProcessTable, process: TerminatedProcess}, Diagnostic>
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/diagnostic.dag: Diagnostic
//   - std/integer.dag: Int (and its Tier-2 totalizations); String kernel-ambient
//   - std/machine.dag: Byte
//   - std/algebra.dag: FreeMonoid
//   - std/collection.dag: List, Map
//   - extdeps/file_system.dag: AbsolutePath (for Command.program)
//
// Status: T-4.5 modeled 2026-05-16 (keen-wren-663). Operation bodies
//   are external realizations owned by the realization/pipeline layer;
//   this file owns the typed carriers and fail-closed operation
//   signatures only.
// Brief: see BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md; this file's I/O contract is the immutable
// contract for the worker. Splitting this file requires explicit operator
// ratification (substrate extension = stop signal).
//
// HEADER RECONCILE (2026-05-16, #3209, keen-wren-663 + operator ruling):
// wait/capture return an advanced TerminatedProcess (single-authority
// terminal fact with Running unrepresentable), reconciling the frozen
// wait/capture contract per the operator P2/§2-floor BLOCKING.
// Follow-up effect-resource reconcile: process
// operations also take an explicit ProcessTable resource parameter per
// ratified lens/effect.dag B3/B-5; spawn/wait/capture return the
// modified ProcessTable alongside the advanced process. D5 also
// reconciles the stale ProcessState::Exited rationale prose above,
// which the same operator-sanctioned terminal-carrier refactor made
// stale. The terminal postcondition reconcile factors terminal process
// state through TerminatedProcessState, so all terminal operation
// carriers reuse the same terminal-state carrier.
// HEADER RECONCILE (2026-05-17, #3209, keen-wren-663 + briansrls
// BLOCKING): Command carries caller-supplied argv0 explicitly. POSIX
// exec does not derive argv[0] from the program path; fabricating it
// would collapse distinct command invocations.
// HEADER RECONCILE (2026-05-17, #3209, keen-wren-663 + briansrls
// BLOCKING): ProcessTable is an opaque resource carrier, not a readable
// String-labeled identity. The effect/resource authority is the threaded
// carrier position in operation signatures, not a label field.
// HEADER RECONCILE (2026-05-17, #3209, keen-wren-663 + codex BLOCKING):
// capture returns raw byte streams (`FreeMonoid<Byte>`), not decoded
// `String`. Text decoding is a separate fallible projection once v4 owns
// encoding/error carriers.
// HEADER RECONCILE (2026-05-17, #3209, keen-wren-663 + codex BLOCKING):
// spawn returns an opaque CapturedOutputPipes source and capture consumes
// it, so stdout/stderr bytes flow from a declared pipe authority rather
// than appearing from Process alone.
// HEADER RECONCILE (2026-05-17, #3209, keen-wren-663 + codex BLOCKING):
// spawn returns a RunningProcess, not the broader lifecycle Process, so a
// successful spawn cannot structurally return an already-terminated
// child. wait/capture consume that live carrier and return
// TerminatedProcess.
// HEADER RECONCILE (2026-05-17, #3209, keen-wren-663 + briansrls
// BLOCKING): RunningProcess carries CapturedOutputPipes inside the live
// process carrier, so capture cannot pair one process with another
// spawn's pipe source or silently omit the pipe authority.
// HEADER RECONCILE (2026-05-17, #3209, keen-wren-663 + briansrls
// BLOCKING): wait preserves the captured-output pipe authority in
// WaitedTerminatedProcess. Waiting reaps lifecycle/table state; it does
// not by itself drain or close stdout/stderr pipes.
// HEADER RECONCILE (2026-05-17, #3209, keen-wren-663 + codex BLOCKING):
// capture consumes a CaptureSource sum, so it can drain pipe authority
// before wait from RunningProcess or after wait from
// WaitedTerminatedProcess. The preserved post-wait pipe authority has a
// declared consumer and does not require an ad hoc side path.
// Follow-up reconcile: wait/capture consume the paired SpawnedProcess /
// WaitedProcess operation carriers instead of dissolving the ProcessTable
// and process handle into parallel request fields.
// HEADER RECONCILE (2026-05-17, #3209, keen-wren-663 + codex BLOCKING):
// the unused broad Process carrier was removed after operation-specific
// lifecycle carriers landed. RunningProcess, WaitedTerminatedProcess,
// and TerminatedProcess are now the only process lifecycle boundary
// carriers in this substrate file.
// Follow-up reconcile: the dead ProcessState sum was also removed; no
// declared lifecycle carrier remains without a structural consumer.
// HEADER RECONCILE (2026-05-17, #3209, keen-wren-663 + openai-pro
// BLOCKING): process identity is factored into ProcessIdentity and
// threaded through RunningProcess / WaitedTerminatedProcess /
// TerminatedProcess. Lifecycle carriers no longer re-list id/command/
// parent as parallel process-identity authorities.


module v4.extdeps.process


import v4.extdeps.file_system { AbsolutePath }
import v4.std.algebra { FreeMonoid }
import v4.std.diagnostic { Outcome }
import v4.std.machine { Byte }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// HOST-PROCESS BOUNDARY IS TYPED. This file is the v4 carrier for the
// INVARIANTS.md P2 host-process boundary discipline: command execution
// returns structural outcomes, not string probes over shared stderr.
// Setup/spawn failure is `Outcome.Rejected { diagnostic }`; a logical
// child that ran and terminated returns an advanced `TerminatedProcess`
// whose `state` carries the terminal fact. The two facts are not
// collapsed, so a wrapper failure cannot be mistaken for a child exit
// and a signal death cannot be fabricated as an exit code.
//
// TERMINATION IS THE SINGLE AUTHORITY. The terminal-status facts
// (`Exited` vs `Signaled`) live only in `Termination`, reached through
// the shared `TerminatedProcessState` carrier. wait returns
// `WaitedTerminatedProcess`, capture returns `TerminatedProcess`, and
// neither result can carry `Running` or a sibling `exit_code` field.
//
// VALUE-REFINEMENT SCAFFOLDS. POSIX PIDs are positive integers, exit
// status values are non-negative and conventionally bounded to 8 bits
// for shell observation, and signal numbers are positive members of the
// platform signal table. v4 currently has `Int` / `UInt` but not the
// refined numeric subset machinery in this dependency path. The aliases
// below are tracked scaffolds with bridge properties:
//   (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; the aliases name the parent numeric
//       carrier but do not yet type-enforce POSIX bounds.
//   (b) bounds on use — producers must construct positive PIDs,
//       non-negative exit codes, and platform-valid signal numbers.
//       Violating those predicates is a producer bug against the model.
//   (c) dissolution trigger — when v4 lands numeric value refinements /
//       bounded subsets over integer carriers, replace these aliases
//       with refined carriers and keep the surrounding sums unchanged.
//
// COMMAND IS DATA, NOT A SHELL STRING. `Command.program` is an
// `AbsolutePath`; argv and env are structured carriers. This prevents
// shell quoting, PATH lookup, env splitting, and diagnostic text from
// becoming hidden authorities. A separate shell-language extdep can
// model shell parsing when the program intentionally invokes a shell;
// this process model does not smuggle one in.
//
// OPERATION SEAM. The scaffold header's `Result<..., Diagnostic>` prose
// denotes std/diagnostic.dag's ratified `Outcome<T>` carrier
// (DECISIONS.md I). Per lens/effect.dag B3/B-5, type signatures are the
// single effect authority: effects are explicit parameters, and the
// effect lens reads returned-modified-resource as write and
// returned-derived-value as read. The external realization layer
// performs fork/exec/wait/capture and returns through these carriers.
//
// CAPTURE OUTPUT IS BYTES. POSIX pipes carry byte streams; decoding
// stdout/stderr to text requires an encoding choice and a fallible error
// carrier that this file does not own. `capture` therefore returns raw
// `FreeMonoid<Byte>` streams. Text projection belongs in a later
// encoding-aware seam.
//
// CAPTURE OUTPUT SOURCE IS STRUCTURAL. `CapturedOutputPipes` is an
// opaque handle to the stdout/stderr pipe arrangement established for a
// spawned process. It is deliberately not a file-descriptor model: fd
// numbering, open/close/dup, and general pipe operations are outside this
// frozen subset. RunningProcess owns the handle so returned bytes have a
// declared source tied to the specific live child.
//
// SPAWN SUCCESS IS LIVE. A successful spawn cannot return a terminal
// child observation. `RunningProcess` is the live postcondition carrier
// for spawn and the input carrier for wait/capture; it carries the
// captured-output pipe source for that child. `WaitedTerminatedProcess`
// carries the same pipe source after wait; `TerminatedProcess` is the
// terminal postcondition carrier after capture has consumed the source.
// `CaptureSource` is the capture input authority: either the pre-wait
// SpawnedProcess pair, or the post-wait WaitedProcess pair.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// POSIX numeric carriers.
// Anchor: https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/basedefs/sys_types.h.html
// Anchor: https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/basedefs/signal.h.html
type ProcessId = Int
type ExitCode = Int
type SignalNum = Int


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Command — the exec-family invocation surface v4 needs.
// Anchor: https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/functions/exec.html
//
// `program` is an absolute filesystem path from extdeps/file_system.dag,
// not a shell string or PATH-search policy.
// `argv0` is caller-supplied POSIX argv[0], not derived from `program`.
// `args` are the remaining argv entries after argv[0]. `env` is a map
// from variable name to value; duplicate variable names are structurally
// unrepresentable.
type Command {
  program: AbsolutePath
  argv0: String
  args: List<String>
  env: Map<String, String>
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Termination.
// Anchor: https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/functions/waitpid.html
// Anchor: https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/basedefs/sys_wait.h.html
//
// `Termination` carries the POSIX terminal-status partition consumed by
// wait/capture. Live state is represented by the separate
// `RunningProcess` carrier; terminal operation outputs carry
// `TerminatedProcessState`, which wraps this sum.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1) — classification:
// 🟡 YELLOW, first-consumer-confirmed. The POSIX wait-status partition is
// structurally grounded here, but the first effect/resolver consumer
// must prove the mapping from raw wait status into `Exited` vs
// `Signaled` without string or integer sentinels. Named trigger:
// T-4.5 effect/resolver seam lands fork/exec/wait realization; that PR
// must either confirm this partition or amend this carrier under review.
// Ledger:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: terminal status is consumed uniformly by
//      wait/capture terminal carriers; not per-consumer metadata.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: Exited carries ExitCode and Signaled
//      carries SignalNum; a flat record admits impossible combinations.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: terminal status is not an algebra carrier.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a terminal process ended by exit OR signal,
//      not both.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the variants are not the same
//      payload shape projected over an enumerable set.
type Termination
  = Exited { code: ExitCode }
  | Signaled { signal: SignalNum }

type TerminatedProcessState {
  termination: Termination
}

// `TerminatedProcessState` is the single terminal-state carrier.
// wait/capture success carriers reference it directly, so terminal
// operation results cannot disagree about the terminal status by
// construction.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Process lifecycle carriers — a spawned child process as known to the parent.
// Anchor: https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/functions/fork.html
//
// `ProcessIdentity` is the single carrier for the child process identity
// facts this file owns. `parent` is the parent process identifier in the
// bounded spawned-child model named by the frozen scaffold. Process
// groups, sessions, orphan reparenting, and general process-table
// observation are outside this file's scope unless a future scaffold
// amends it.
// `RunningProcess` is the spawn success and wait/capture input carrier.
// Its lifecycle state is fixed by the carrier itself: a successful spawn
// cannot be already terminated. The captured-output pipe source is
// inside this carrier, so it cannot be separated from the live child it
// belongs to.
type ProcessIdentity {
  id: ProcessId
  command: Command
  parent: ProcessId
}

type RunningProcess {
  identity: ProcessIdentity
  output: CapturedOutputPipes
}

// `TerminatedProcess` is the capture success carrier. Its state is the
// shared terminal-state carrier, so `Running` is structurally impossible
// in successful capture results.
type TerminatedProcess {
  identity: ProcessIdentity
  state: TerminatedProcessState
}

// `WaitedTerminatedProcess` is the wait success carrier. It is terminal
// like `TerminatedProcess`, but preserves the captured-output pipe source
// that was established at spawn. Waiting alone observes/reaps process
// state; it does not drain or close captured stdout/stderr.
type WaitedTerminatedProcess {
  identity: ProcessIdentity
  state: TerminatedProcessState
  output: CapturedOutputPipes
}

// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1) — classification:
// 🟡 YELLOW, first-consumer-confirmed. Capture can drain output from a
// still-running spawned child or from a child whose lifecycle was already
// waited while preserving pipe authority. The first capture realization
// consumer must confirm both paths use the paired operation carrier and
// do not route through an ambient process table side channel. Named
// trigger: T-4.5 effect/resolver seam lands capture realization.
// Ledger:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: capture source state is consumed
//      uniformly by capture, not per-consumer metadata.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: each variant carries the lifecycle
//      operation carrier that owns ProcessTable plus CapturedOutputPipes
//      for that state.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: capture source is not an algebra carrier.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: one capture request uses either live or
//      post-wait authority, not both.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the variants are different
//      lifecycle carriers, not a projection over identical payloads.
type CaptureSource
  = Live { spawned: SpawnedProcess }
  | Waited { waited: WaitedProcess }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Operation contract carriers.
// Anchor: https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/functions/fork.html
// Anchor: https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/functions/exec.html
// Anchor: https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/functions/waitpid.html
//
// `ProcessTable` is the explicit opaque resource parameter for this
// extdep's process-table effects. It has no readable payload: resource
// identity is threaded structurally through operation signatures, and the
// effect fact is read from each signature.
type ProcessTable

// Spawn failure, wait failure, and capture failure return `Rejected`
// with a typed `Diagnostic`; a successfully observed child termination
// returns the advanced `TerminatedProcess` whose terminal status is
// structurally present. stdout/stderr are captured as separate raw byte
// streams so harness/setup diagnostics cannot be
// blended into the logical child's output channel. The
// `ProcessOperations` record is the declared callable boundary a future
// host realization must inhabit; it is intentionally only a signature
// bundle here, not an effect resolver.
type CapturedByteStream = FreeMonoid<Byte>

type CapturedOutputPipes

type SpawnProcessRequest {
  table: ProcessTable
  command: Command
}

type SpawnedProcess {
  table: ProcessTable
  process: RunningProcess
}

type SpawnProcessResult = Outcome<SpawnedProcess>

type WaitProcessRequest {
  spawned: SpawnedProcess
}

type WaitedProcess {
  table: ProcessTable
  process: WaitedTerminatedProcess
}

type WaitProcessResult = Outcome<WaitedProcess>

type CapturedProcessOutput {
  stdout: CapturedByteStream
  stderr: CapturedByteStream
  table: ProcessTable
  process: TerminatedProcess
}

type CaptureProcessRequest {
  source: CaptureSource
}

type CaptureProcessResult = Outcome<CapturedProcessOutput>

type ProcessOperations {
  spawn: fn(SpawnProcessRequest) -> SpawnProcessResult
  wait: fn(WaitProcessRequest) -> WaitProcessResult
  capture: fn(CaptureProcessRequest) -> CaptureProcessResult
}
